1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,460
In 1324 a king from West Africa rode into
Cairo and gave away so much gold that,

2
00:00:06,460 --> 00:00:11,420
one chronicler says, the price of
gold stayed low for twelve years.

3
00:00:11,760 --> 00:00:16,180
He was Mansa Musa, the ruler
of Mali, on his way to Mecca.

4
00:00:16,500 --> 00:00:19,500
His kingdom taxed the
trade in gold and salt,

5
00:00:19,500 --> 00:00:23,020
and West Africa supplied much of the
gold that reached the Mediterranean.

6
00:00:23,360 --> 00:00:27,000
He arrived in July, camped for
three days beside the pyramids,

7
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:29,660
and stayed in Cairo three months.

8
00:00:29,900 --> 00:00:33,440
The sultan, al-Nasir,
sent an emir to meet him.

9
00:00:33,480 --> 00:00:37,920
That emir later told what he saw
to a historian named al-Umari.

10
00:00:38,200 --> 00:00:42,480
Musa spoke to him only through an
interpreter, though his Arabic was perfect.

11
00:00:42,580 --> 00:00:46,180
He sent many loads of raw
gold to the sultan's treasury.

12
00:00:46,180 --> 00:00:50,440
But when the emir urged him to visit
the sultan himself, he refused.

13
00:00:50,580 --> 00:00:54,220
"I came for the pilgrimage
and nothing else," he said.

14
00:00:54,480 --> 00:00:55,840
The emir understood.

15
00:00:55,960 --> 00:00:59,020
At court, Musa would
have to kiss the ground.

16
00:00:59,300 --> 00:01:02,900
The emir kept pressing,
and at last Musa agreed.

17
00:01:03,180 --> 00:01:06,660
In the sultan's presence they
told him: kiss the ground.

18
00:01:06,940 --> 00:01:09,340
"How may this be?" said Musa.

19
00:01:09,480 --> 00:01:11,780
A man beside him whispered something.

20
00:01:12,020 --> 00:01:16,440
"I make obeisance to God who
created me," he said, and bowed.

21
00:01:16,700 --> 00:01:20,140
The sultan half rose and
sat him by his own side.

22
00:01:20,340 --> 00:01:22,300
Then he began to give.

23
00:01:22,560 --> 00:01:26,340
"He flooded Cairo with his
benefactions," the emir said.

24
00:01:26,400 --> 00:01:29,560
"He left no court emir, nor
holder of a royal office,

25
00:01:29,560 --> 00:01:32,100
without the gift of a load of gold."

26
00:01:32,400 --> 00:01:35,620
The people of Cairo bought and
sold with Musa and his men,

27
00:01:35,620 --> 00:01:38,040
and made incalculable profits.

28
00:01:38,220 --> 00:01:42,960
They exchanged gold, the emir said,
until its value in Egypt fell.

29
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:44,900
Al-Umari had numbers.

30
00:01:45,060 --> 00:01:48,640
Before Musa, a mithqal of
gold, a standard weight,

31
00:01:48,640 --> 00:01:52,160
was never worth less than
twenty-five silver dirhams.

32
00:01:52,160 --> 00:01:54,780
Afterward, never more than twenty-two.

33
00:01:54,840 --> 00:01:59,600
That had been so, he wrote, "for
about twelve years until this day."

34
00:01:59,880 --> 00:02:02,120
Whether it is true is another matter.

35
00:02:02,220 --> 00:02:04,940
The twelve years come from al-Umari alone.

36
00:02:05,160 --> 00:02:10,000
Another chronicler, al-Maqrizi, says
the gold dinar lost six dirhams.

37
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:14,620
And the modern historian Warren Schultz,
comparing recorded exchange rates,

38
00:02:14,620 --> 00:02:18,500
found changes well within the
normal swings of Mamluk Egypt.

39
00:02:18,780 --> 00:02:21,860
What the gold did cost was Musa himself.

40
00:02:22,140 --> 00:02:26,040
On the road back from Mecca, many
of his pilgrims died of cold,

41
00:02:26,040 --> 00:02:29,540
hunger and bandits, and much
of his baggage was lost.

42
00:02:29,640 --> 00:02:32,120
With his money gone, he
borrowed from Cairo's

43
00:02:32,120 --> 00:02:34,620
merchants and sold what
he had bought there.

44
00:02:34,620 --> 00:02:39,000
Al-Umari and Ibn Khaldun both say
the lenders were never repaid,

45
00:02:39,040 --> 00:02:42,640
or only in part, though
other sources disagree.

46
00:02:43,260 --> 00:02:45,040
The story kept traveling.

47
00:02:45,340 --> 00:02:49,380
Around 1375, a mapmaker
on Majorca drew him

48
00:02:49,380 --> 00:02:52,640
in the Catalan Atlas,
with gold in his hand.

49
00:02:52,800 --> 00:02:57,700
The caption calls him "the richest
and noblest of all these lands."

50
00:02:58,120 --> 00:03:01,780
Today he is called the
richest man who ever lived.

51
00:03:02,040 --> 00:03:06,780
The mapmaker said something
smaller: richest of those lands.

52
00:03:07,100 --> 00:03:11,120
So nobody can say for certain what
happened to the price of gold.

53
00:03:11,380 --> 00:03:14,440
But al-Umari tells us how his story began.

54
00:03:14,780 --> 00:03:19,460
From the day he came to live in Egypt, he
writes, he heard talk of the arrival of

55
00:03:19,460 --> 00:03:24,840
the sultan Musa, and found the people of
Cairo eager to tell him what they had seen.

56
00:03:25,340 --> 00:03:30,140
Gold comes and goes, but the story
of the king who gave it away stayed.

57
00:03:30,140 --> 00:03:33,862
If you like forgotten stories
from across the medieval world,

58
00:03:33,862 --> 00:03:36,840
leave a like and subscribe
to Animated Tales.

59
00:03:36,840 --> 00:03:38,671
Until next time.
